e each other  they'll be trying to find scapegoats  he'll be paranoid and anxious there are  reports that two very senior members of  the fsb which is russia's domestic  security service the main successor to  the kgb have been placed under house  arrest and these two individuals  um uh were both involved closely in  planning for the invasion so that gives  you a sense of uh the sort of mood in  moscow in the sense of anxiety if you  think of the mechanism of a coup  um  what what is it how does this come about  well it comes about when  some people who are close to power  think that their time is up and they  think that someone is about to move  against them  and that if they don't act first they'll  end up in jail or dead  and so that's why this is such an  unstable moment because the moment at  which blame is being cast is the moment  which everybody's looking to see whether  they're the ones who are going to end up  carrying the can for this disaster and  if they if they're convinced they are  then that's when they move and they try  and move before they're arrested so  this erect this house arrest it is like  the starting pistol firing and we'll see  how it unfolds  [Music]  there's two sorts of risks one of them  is that the war going badly compels  vladimir putin to use something like  chemical weapons  and that creates intense pressures on  western governments to intervene and of  course when the syrian regime used  chemical weapons in 2018 the us uk and  france conducted airstrikes against them  that won't happen in this case it won't  happen and i think although the no-fly  zone debate will revive i think  ultimately there will be no appetite for  the biden administration or in europe to  seriously contemplate shooting down  russian planes or blowing up russian air  defense systems  however there are other sorts of  interventions we could see so for  example could we see a debate over  whether nato should send uh chemical  biological radiological unit protective  units to a site where chemical weapons  have been used i ultimately think any  combat forces on ukrainian soil in a way  that may drag us into confrontation is  still a very very low probability  outcome but the pressures for that  outcome will grow in the aftermath of  such an atrocity but the second  possibility even without the use of  chemical weapons is a scenario in which  vladimir putin decides that western arms  supplies are causing him too many  problems that this is a this represents  western intervention and that he  attempts to strike a arms depot in  poland or simply strike a arms convoy  moving into ukraine in the western side  of that country  those sorts of things could easily  involve escalation to a nato-russia  conventional clash and then that could  spiral into russian nuclear use that  would create pressures for western  nuclear use we shouldn't exaggerate  those risks i think with the west  western governments are absolutely keen  to stay out of this conflict directly  but nor should we be complacent about  those risks because they they do exist  uh and these are these are the certainly  the highest prospect of uh war between  russia and the west we have seen since  the 1980s if not before  [Music]  my best case scenario is that this does  not actually escalate into the use of  chemical or nuclear weapons of mass  destructions  so whatever the peace settlement is my  uh absolutely best hope is that uh would  come out and it will not come out well  nobody will come out of well uh of this  any time soon i don't think but  minimizing and not letting it into a  stage of complete catastrophe would be  my best  hope at the moment you know ukrainian  morale self-belief  is hugely important in their effort  to counter russian attacks  and one way to destroy morale i think  putin's hoping for in an old way it goes  back to the 19th century when russian  forces used artillery against cities  is to try and kill civilians and to  make people miserable and i think if you  do that you present um zinski with the  the ukrainian president with a decision  which is  do do you go for peace do you settle in  some way with russia in order to spare  your people  and by killing people now in  indiscriminate ways and in large volumes  you present zelensky with that very  difficult choice  for me the best outcome is  a negotiated settlement  that leaves um the zelinski government  in place  in which zelensky willingly  makes  some agreements such as recognizing  russia's hold on crimea  and says he's not going to join nato but  he willingly does it this is not  something that's imposed on him  and then  that  russian forces withdraw  including from the all the areas they've  occupied  and i think that has  that's  that has two advantages  one is  that the ukrainians themselves having  forged a nation in this crisis would  then be free to go their way and i think  their way leads towards the west  and the second point is that i don't  think a russian leader especially not  vladimir putin if they do that will  invade  again thank you for watching for all our  coverage on the war in ukraine please  click on the link and don't forget to  subscribe

